    <h2>What "calorie needs" means</h2>
    <p>Your body uses energy in 3 main ways: keeping you alive at rest (basal metabolic rate, or BMR, usually the largest share), digesting food, and moving, which includes both exercise and everyday activity like walking and standing. The <a href="../../calculators/tdee.html">TDEE calculator</a> estimates the first with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, then multiplies by an activity factor to approximate the total.</p>

    <h2>Why the estimate is a starting point</h2>
    <p>Equations predict the average for people with your age, sex, height, and weight. Individuals differ: muscle mass, genetics, sleep, and daily movement outside the gym can move real needs 10% or more from the prediction. The activity factor is the largest source of error, because it compresses a whole week of movement into a single number. Most people find the lower activity setting closer to reality.</p>
    <p>The most reliable number comes from your own data. Eating consistently while watching your weekly average weight for a few weeks shows where your maintenance level really is, and any adjustments are better made in small steps.</p>

    <h2>Beyond total calories</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Protein</strong> helps preserve muscle, especially with strength training and as people age. The calculator shows a common target of 1.6 g per kilogram of body weight for active adults.</li>
      <li><strong>Fiber, fruits, and vegetables</strong> don't appear in a calorie total but matter for long-term health and for feeling full.</li>
      <li><strong>Consistency</strong> over weeks matters more than precision on any single day.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When to get personal advice</h2>
    <p>Calorie calculators are designed for healthy adults. Pregnancy and breastfeeding, adolescence, diabetes, kidney disease, and a history of disordered eating all change what is appropriate, and very low intakes carry real risks. A registered dietitian or physician can set targets that fit your health, medications, and goals.</p>
  </div>
